# What it does

[TypeSafe](https://docs.typesafe.ai) models such as Jev answer *questions about
state* with calibrated probabilities instead of generated text. There are three
kinds of question:

- **noul**: a yes/no question. The answer is the probability of yes.
- **choice**: pick one option from a set you define.
- **score**: rate against ordered levels you define.

`tai` wraps the [HTTP API](https://docs.typesafe.ai/api) with a typed DSL.
You describe your questions as a Zig struct literal, and `tai` works out the
result type at compile time. Choice options become a real `enum`, so a
`switch` on the answer is checked for exhaustiveness.

[]($code.siteAsset('snippets/triage.zig').language('zig'))

# Install

`tai` targets Zig `0.17.0-dev` and has no dependencies beyond the standard
library.

```sh
zig fetch --save git+https://github.com/benomahony/zig-typesafe-ai
```

Then, in `build.zig`:

[]($code.siteAsset('snippets/install-build.zig').language('zig'))

# Quick start

Get an API key from TypeSafe and export it:

```sh
export TYPESAFE_API_KEY=...
```

[]($code.siteAsset('snippets/quickstart.zig').language('zig'))

`ask` returns a plain value that owns no heap memory, so there is nothing to
free.

# Every example is a test

Every Zig example on this site is quoted from an end-to-end test that runs
against the live API (`tests/e2e.zig`), or from a real downstream project
(`tests/consumer`) that depends on `tai` exactly as shown above. If an
example stops working, the build fails.

